en and you can't do it you  know your body doesn't cooperate it's a  really profound heartache you know for  people it's a source of real profound  suffering and so I started you know  looking at infertility in Egypt in in  the late 1980s and it was just at the  beginning of in vitro fertilization ivf  making a test tube baby had just become  possible in the UK and i really started  following the spread of ivf into the  muslim Middle East I'm a scholar of the  Middle East and so I've been really  studying the impact of IVF in places  like Egypt Lebanon and the United Arab  Emirates which is the topic of my most  recent book ok so I would imagine that  it is a painful topic for many people  I'm curious to know how you did the  research for the book yes so for this  particular book called cosmopolitan  conceptions IVF sojourn Xin global Dubai  I was interested in Dubai one of the  seven united arab emirates and it's a is  really a global city now with about two  million people in it and it's become a  kind of hub what i'm calling a repro hub  or a medical tourism hub for people  coming from really about a third of the  world's nations flying into dubai to try  to access medical care and in this case  IVF and so I ended up situating myself  in what I call a kind of cosmopolitan  clinic a clinic called conceived in the  emirate in Dubai actually on the border  of Dubai in the neighboring emirate of  sharjah and it was a clinic that had  really developed a kind of global  reputation as a safe affordable  effective place to go seek IVF and so I  literally in staying in this clinic for  about half a year I literally  interviewed about 220 people I call  repro travelers coming from 50 different  countries around the world i'm seeking  ivf okay and they had no problem  speaking with you know you know for the  most part because they had been through  a very sort of tortuous journeys to get  there they had you know often it was a  place of last resort they had spent a  lot of money they had struggled so much  to get to this point  usually they wanted to speak to me about  their sort of travails their journeys  their sojourn why they had come to this  particular clinic and so no I ended up  really you know talking to men women and  couples together from really all over  the world i would say every part of the  world was represented except for latin  america and the caribbean because that's  very far away from Dubai but there were  people coming from Europe from the UK  from the US Australia many people coming  from other Middle Eastern countries and  many people coming from parts of Asia  South Asia India Pakistan Sri Lanka  Malaysia Indonesia so it's really you  know it was a sight of global these kind  of Cosmopolitan conceptions as I came to  think of it I am wondering why dubai of  all places is it so much cheaper i mean  when you mentioned people from the  united states in the UK and europe going  that's surprising to me so why do you  think dubai is emerging as this medical  repro hub yeah part of it is statecraft  part of it is due by trying to become a  place of medical care a kind of global  medical tourism site I mean Dubai  specializes in international tourism  it's a global tourism and shopping hub  but they have actually developed  something called Dubai healthcare city  which we would call a med asst it II a  kind of small tax free zone where they  have you know hundreds of medical  professionals and clinics and people can  travel there the clinic I was working in  was not located in Dubai healthcare city  but the point is that Dubai is trying to  develop a sort of global medical tourism  reputation and because of that people  think of Dubai particularly in certain  parts of the world is the kind of go to  place to get a sort of higher quality of  medical care than they can access in  their home countries and so for example  there were many people coming from the  eastern coasts of Africa sub-saharan  Africa people coming from Somalia  Djibouti Tanzania where there are no IVF  clinics and so for them it's sort of  proximity and this kind of reputation of  being a place where you could safely  access health care for people coming  from the west and there were many people  in my study coming from the West it's  not that it was less expensive in fact  Dubai actually  offers rather expensive IVF it costs  about 5000 to 6000 US dollars to do a  cycle of IVF in the UAE but that is less  than half of the price of a cycle of IVF  in the United States the u.s. is the  most expensive place in the world to do  IVF the average hears about 12,000 US  dollars and most people don't have  health insurance to cover it IVF is not  considered to be something that it's  considered to be a kind of elective  procedure rather than a medical  necessity and so in the US that's not  covered and so people really have to  spend a lot of money to try to access it  in this country so yes some people from  the US will actually go travel to other  countries to try to access less costly  more affordable IVF care and you're in  the prologue of you of your book you  write about Rania why did you choose to  write about her and tell us a little bit  about her reproductive journey yes so I  decided to focus on the story a very  poignant story in a very kind of  capacious very fully detailed story of a  woman who I call Rania it's not a real  name she was an Ethiopian Eritrean  refugees to the UK she had left as a  teen to escape you know political  violence and was a refugee and now a  citizen of the United Kingdom and she  had met another refugee a Sudanese  refugee and they had married and they  had a very tumultuous path they had  trouble getting pregnant she had tried  eight IVF cycles in the UK I mean under  the National Health Service in the UK  actually will subsidise cycles for  people and so she had tried you know  subsidized IVF in the UK which hadn't  worked she'd gone to the private ivf  sector in the UK and through all of that  she had ended up with one daughter and  being an African woman you know in her  community in the UK she lived in a kind  of african community in the UK she was  stigmatized as being a woman really  without children she had one daughter  but you know in her society people had  four or five children and she  desperately wanted to have at least one  more child to be a sibling to her her  daughter sure and so through a variety  of circumstances she ended up coming to  this clinic called conceived in she came  to Dubai  hoping to try to get access to IVF that  she could afford and would be safe and  she had a very difficult time she  unbeknownst to her had a reproductive  tract infection that was sort of inside  her body and when she came to the UAE to  do another a 9th ivf cycle it sort of  released this hidden infection and she  became septic she had sort of a full  infection that was life-threatening they  were able to treat it you know cure it  and sent her back to the UK and said you  know you need to have parts of your  reproductive body actually removed  because they're infected and and they're  hurting you more than helping you and  still still wanting to have a child she  came back to Dubai she came back to the  UAE and she was successful she did two  more tries of IVF there and she ended up  having two more children a daughter and  a son the last part of her story which I  stood highlight in the end of the book  she tried one more time in the UAE when  she was about 43 years of age it was her  13th ivf cycle it didn't work and so she  didn't get you know another child but  she ended up having three IVF children  through incredible determination  thousands of you know dollars and to  sort of have a normative life as a  mother of children mm-hmm okay so let's  talk about the main reasons people  travel to places like Dubai for I via  yeah there's sort of four main reasons  why people to travel and I'll just sort  of go through them briefly one 